28: * A sheet's pools, each name once, and their members: people of the sheet, each at most once in a
29: * pool, and other pools, each a member of at most one pool, with a share percent. A person can be in
30: * several pools. A pool cannot contain itself through any chain, and a chain of pools is at most three
31: * deep. A pool is balanced when its shares
32: * total exactly 100.00; a retired member's share (retirement date set, whatever the date) counts toward
33: * the total as given. Each member of a balanced pool cuts the pool's pot (none: no cuts) - the sheet's
34: * pot for a pool that is a member of none, else its cut in the pool above: the pot
35: * times their share over the shares of the members with a cut, rounded half to even to two decimals, a
36: * retired member, or a person with a hold in force on the sheet's today, 0.00 (no cut); the remainder, the
37: * pot less the cuts, goes to the member with the smallest non-zero cut, the latest added on a tie (a member
38: * with no cut gets none; with no non-zero cut, to the last member with a share), so the cuts total exactly the pot (all 0.00 when no member has a cut). A retired member's
39: * share is so spread over the others in proportion to their shares, as a held member's is; a pool whose
40: * every member (whose dates include the sheet's today) is retired has no cuts. A hold leaves the
41: * total share and balance as they are. A membership may carry a from-date and a to-date (both included);
42: * a member whose dates do not include the sheet's today is left out entirely: its share counts 0.00 toward
43: * the total and it has no cut. A crew is also a pool, listed after the pools under its name (no pool has a crew's
44: * name): its lead and then its members (people still on the sheet), at equal shares of 100.00 split as patrons'
45: * shares are - each rounded half to even, the remainder on the lead - cut like any pool's members. Its members
46: * come only from the crew.
47: */
